#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133617 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "234800a9-ae9c-56c4-8284-84041f129a59" :question/math-academy-id "e-8759" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy^2+ 2xz.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle e^{t}, t, e^{-t}\\rangle$ for $t\\in \\left[0,1\\right],$ evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨e,1,\\frac{1}{e}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,1)$ and $\\left(e, 1, \\dfrac{1}{e}\\right).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e,1,\\frac{1}{e})-f(1,0,1) \\\\  & =(e(1)^{2}+2\\cdot e\\cdot \\frac{1}{e})-(1(0)^{2}+2(1)(1)) \\\\  & =(e+2)-2 \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133618 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/id #uuid "667923e0-14bc-5bbd-8129-333703cd6616" :question/math-academy-id "e-8760" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t,  1-t \\right\\rangle, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int _C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r}$ for $\\mathbf F = ye^{xy}\\,\\mathbf i + xe^{xy}\\,\\mathbf j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf F = \\nabla f.$ Since we're given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =y{e}^{xy}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)= e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,0)-f(0,1) \\\\  & ={e}^{1\\cdot 0}-{e}^{0\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133629 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "3f724b05-571d-529d-ab2b-db9dd6d2b531" :question/math-academy-id "e-9502" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)= xe^{y}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $x + 2y=3$ for $x\\in [-1,1],$ evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\dfrac{1}{2}(3-t)\\right\\rangle, \\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(-1)) \\\\  & =f(1,1)-f(-1,2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot {e}^{1}-(-1)\\cdot {e}^{2} \\\\  & =e+{e}^{2} \\\\  & =e(1+e).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "504cb021-2dc3-5db0-ba79-128d9b4f9867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337474} {:answer/id #uuid "f589185d-0128-571a-99b9-6127a6f05031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337475} {:answer/id #uuid "6b05b441-9d42-562c-87d9-8781c08a7b3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337476} {:answer/id #uuid "0e70f472-a624-5040-ab16-34d1e6379894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337477} {:answer/id #uuid "a96bcabd-de03-5e05-bef7-ae66aece6e73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e70f472-a624-5040-ab16-34d1e6379894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337477} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d28a1bb-c094-553f-b16c-0efb855f6537" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9b52684-6943-5924-8a3c-2cbafe021e4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104617" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for  $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(0+1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9822f971-0b1f-52cb-b48f-042d04eedb10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337481} {:answer/id #uuid "21747ef9-b305-51dd-a795-b78d43798a50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337482} {:answer/id #uuid "b476aafd-cd2a-5b74-a694-a9d5925a6de3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337483} {:answer/id #uuid "1703eca6-ca8c-5467-8632-b3013fa71d87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337484} {:answer/id #uuid "6341392c-f72f-5eda-a514-6019d889a687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6341392c-f72f-5eda-a514-6019d889a687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337485} :answer-field/id #uuid "35da032d-c9af-5b8b-86a4-f702c895bffe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d33ad9d6-364b-5f5c-8112-5f5c1da01d95" :question/math-academy-id "q-104623" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}\\cdot \\pi )-({0}^{2}\\cdot 0-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =(2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d40e8f9a-cc43-50c9-8b44-7449fc359e3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{3}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337488} {:answer/id #uuid "ea61c170-f603-5b21-b049-2fed1dce609e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337489} {:answer/id #uuid "d865b04d-9d85-56c7-acd4-46dc1ecb40a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337490} {:answer/id #uuid "3a68fbb3-a3d5-5d01-8a31-b0714ca2befc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡2+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337491} {:answer/id #uuid "334c4b6b-ed2b-5297-9f15-c5fbe2215313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{ln}^{3}⁡2-63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea61c170-f603-5b21-b049-2fed1dce609e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337489} :answer-field/id #uuid "b940db30-d4ac-58ed-9bdd-6c9560dc490b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8896c3f5-9f61-59e4-9099-67d4914ed9e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-104733" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=({x}^{2}-y)\\,i+({y}^{2}-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot 4)-(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}-0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =9{ln}^{3}⁡2+\\frac{64}{3}-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "526f7552-a18a-5e0d-924f-6a2a9e5cdc98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337495} {:answer/id #uuid "2b56c420-3592-5ff4-9ed2-d66ecebd37d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337496} {:answer/id #uuid "c963e1f9-d5e8-5c5c-a72d-7160476c3bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337497} {:answer/id #uuid "b7edbd2c-c0d9-50d1-ae32-800849d75c41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337498} {:answer/id #uuid "60a4bb50-1405-5ba3-9b0f-ed0daf2ac52d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "526f7552-a18a-5e0d-924f-6a2a9e5cdc98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337495} :answer-field/id #uuid "c70b6be5-6032-58b8-a7bc-73d5c1bc3f0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72b1f81d-cb45-57b0-98cf-3032506f331a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104739" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y})\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y})}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-({0}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(0+{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b5193e-4d3a-5a68-9935-ab53b74b41a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337502} {:answer/id #uuid "21ca5670-1ec6-56f0-b1be-827cf09aa7f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337503} {:answer/id #uuid "0827866f-4490-56da-8495-2a7a9dd3ba93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337504} {:answer/id #uuid "bfffa47d-ab9d-5c4e-af0c-142558b126dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337505} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a2648c-6304-52a3-bc17-35cd577a3403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1a2648c-6304-52a3-bc17-35cd577a3403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337506} :answer-field/id #uuid "4624752d-a286-530b-8f21-e6e14a5665f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81e5d171-627c-5e94-a5b8-312641107175" :question/math-academy-id "q-104745" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=({e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y=x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{({e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,\\pi -1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(\\pi ,\\pi -1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,\\pi -1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\pi \\cdot {e}^{\\pi -1}+(\\pi -1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(0\\cdot {e}^{1}+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}+0)-0 \\\\  & =\\pi {e}^{\\pi -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76c445c2-a50e-5b23-b4d3-76c1801091e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337509} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddb5671-0de7-52bc-8316-3297e6f1a1f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2e-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337510} {:answer/id #uuid "4991d7f0-98fd-5667-9afb-016a4e2962b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337511} {:answer/id #uuid "ae86b783-8132-527b-80a7-7bfbeffe2201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡e-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337512} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2b22a5-88cc-5dab-a28e-1c5f16cee8f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76c445c2-a50e-5b23-b4d3-76c1801091e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337509} :answer-field/id #uuid "a003a062-911c-5434-b258-0b0cceabcf8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6e3d391f-7795-50e6-b816-0c7d52038f87" :question/math-academy-id "q-104747" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡e)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡e-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "690646f5-c7b7-5e45-94fd-1788aaebbf7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{ln}^{2}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337516} {:answer/id #uuid "01f97d20-d223-5989-92e3-107a070088af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337517} {:answer/id #uuid "729767aa-1ee8-565d-97b2-1cb4e8f4335e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337518} {:answer/id #uuid "b528c2f9-76d0-5505-a68c-a5eb60923cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337519} {:answer/id #uuid "faf55122-699b-512c-beff-cf073616e47f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "faf55122-699b-512c-beff-cf073616e47f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337520} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaf821bc-7700-5009-b487-8ae8247fc5aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e5ae920f-cea9-5c2f-a972-f3194327bb51" :question/math-academy-id "q-104761" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2x{e}^{-y}\\,i+(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ for $x\\in [2,3].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{2x{e}^{-y}}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x{e}^{-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x{e}^{-y}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{-y}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)⟩,\\,t\\in [2,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨2,0⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(2,0)$ and $(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(2,0) \\\\  & =({3}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2})-({2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-0}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9fe3e30-c701-50fb-9192-ed3819d71c76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130576} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b0acbf-8d9e-57a4-ab15-f6a78d1d18d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130577} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e8c28f-1e3b-5815-beb9-c8bad1f7d399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130578} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc453d2-c280-5b2f-a9c6-409a8b796314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130579} {:answer/id #uuid "6970c568-7f36-5365-8ddb-b3ab5ed2f214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6e8c28f-1e3b-5815-beb9-c8bad1f7d399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130578} :answer-field/id #uuid "e74b70e0-e939-59ad-8fbd-0493b8527127" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1d55b2c-030e-51c0-add3-e51bc195b461" :question/math-academy-id "q-123879" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve ${y}^{2}=4x$ for $y\\in [0,2\\sqrt{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\sqrt{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(2\\sqrt{2}) & =⟨2,2\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2\\sqrt{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,2\\sqrt{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =((2\\sqrt{2})^{2}-{2}^{2})-({0}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =(8-4)-(0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337521 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94795672-8c2f-5130-b2d0-dec335f39fa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337523} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ab7373-d9e5-53a9-9277-9996961af6d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337524} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed5f599-c08a-54b8-88ee-f068bf1394b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337525} {:answer/id #uuid "93be323d-17d4-5899-b0cd-2dc4ef7cfa3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "106" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337526} {:answer/id #uuid "00a163a5-1e5f-52cc-96cb-34833c75953e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93be323d-17d4-5899-b0cd-2dc4ef7cfa3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "106" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337526} :answer-field/id #uuid "01e3ebea-3131-573b-8e40-6be87b5d2424" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4fb6e7d-c5b7-572c-86c1-c0acf17b6a36" :question/math-academy-id "q-124002" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t},t+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,4],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ r(4) & =⟨2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,5).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,5)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({2}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}+2+5)-({0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+0+1) \\\\  & =107-1 \\\\  & =106.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "257e6a34-f475-5d2d-ae92-57452572b71b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337530} {:answer/id #uuid "5b86d8d6-43e2-5f3d-94b9-48ef9f0861b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337531} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad88dd7-9917-52b7-8d2d-9f5e560d164b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337532} {:answer/id #uuid "764333d9-fa4a-5fc4-965e-a72d204ab619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337533} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a982d2-0998-5427-9cf6-2adf5f18f7ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "257e6a34-f475-5d2d-ae92-57452572b71b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337530} :answer-field/id #uuid "e04a934f-c77e-5687-a8d9-2159bd730329" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c94f4d0-20f5-502e-9b9f-6287280afcb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-124003" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{e}^{2x}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,3{t}^{2}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(3{e}^{2\\cdot 1})-(0\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0bbea5b-86ce-52f6-93c3-efd67e6e3134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337537} {:answer/id #uuid "68bc65b4-07f4-54e1-933c-65e2784113cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337538} {:answer/id #uuid "9d177289-183f-5892-b121-f6df81c91dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337539} {:answer/id #uuid "98dbd8da-0b2d-5e2f-9125-74ce3c44c230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337540} {:answer/id #uuid "9b1e6b60-1906-5b7a-aae0-61380962284e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0bbea5b-86ce-52f6-93c3-efd67e6e3134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337537} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aae9b52-3998-53ec-aae2-5cf3c9b61ebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7013c92e-8038-5b66-97de-63bbdbfdb221" :question/math-academy-id "q-124005" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,2t⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ) \\\\  & =(2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\pi )-(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3a34290-e575-5837-9efe-ec2f65217509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337544} {:answer/id #uuid "065ca749-01a0-5343-b8bb-e3eba4a1ecfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337545} {:answer/id #uuid "8e606657-3044-5906-9a8a-f101f61572c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337546} {:answer/id #uuid "e389a045-5d46-5027-8d5f-ba55d798f3fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337547} {:answer/id #uuid "33dca531-d509-5079-ba36-ef6032eb1d74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e389a045-5d46-5027-8d5f-ba55d798f3fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337547} :answer-field/id #uuid "32a67162-40df-535c-a089-1374622367f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b84022c-155d-5594-8712-c795a127d2ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-124008" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+{e}^{z}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2},2{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,1,2)-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =({e}^{1}+{e}^{1}+{e}^{2})-({e}^{0}+{e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(2e+{e}^{2})-3 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2e-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76f80bc6-b64c-5e69-9cd5-c31404ea7848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337551} {:answer/id #uuid "079a94dd-65cb-5f65-8f3f-0baedc6989d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337552} {:answer/id #uuid "39099231-0c3d-5a11-8a68-cbe52c64c2ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337553} {:answer/id #uuid "39222d4a-b564-5066-b817-07b4c9517222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337554} {:answer/id #uuid "c4548094-3fff-562b-aa96-fa3be84bede2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4548094-3fff-562b-aa96-fa3be84bede2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337555} :answer-field/id #uuid "44a93798-762e-56ff-bfd8-119414ed4f09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9108600d-d502-5925-a662-bd43df3a26bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-124029" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve ${y}^{2}=x$ for $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e00351c-154e-5f00-830e-9f21d11d242b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{16}+16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337558} {:answer/id #uuid "6273403b-8b52-5890-8003-da6df730eaec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{16}+{e}^{4}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337559} {:answer/id #uuid "adbed09b-a536-5455-8d30-2f1555e14b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337560} {:answer/id #uuid "c288d06b-1c5f-5646-82c7-7085020a717e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-{e}^{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337561} {:answer/id #uuid "768fb61a-94e5-5769-ae50-bdededd9cbd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adbed09b-a536-5455-8d30-2f1555e14b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337560} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fedfe95-c69e-527f-aa62-88847979916e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c26944f8-c26a-581e-af36-60ef1c01800e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124030" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\sqrt{y}$ for $y\\in [1,4],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t},t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,4].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩, \\\\ r(4) & =⟨4,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(4,4)-f(2,1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}+{e}^{4\\cdot 4})-({2}^{2}+{e}^{2\\cdot 1}) \\\\  & =(16+{e}^{16})-(4+{e}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e02cf51-7b56-59cf-a2d6-aa185409e0a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337565} {:answer/id #uuid "19aa44bd-1db2-58d8-98ba-1d87267c04af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337566} {:answer/id #uuid "8fad278b-6872-5682-b7d8-31ee77033d78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337567} {:answer/id #uuid "45bd400e-54db-5d8e-8a60-ba1a9010e45f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337568} {:answer/id #uuid "efbdcde0-4fec-5fd1-bb55-9299434bf962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45bd400e-54db-5d8e-8a60-ba1a9010e45f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337568} :answer-field/id #uuid "b98d9688-2730-5c00-97b0-8c5ed274e5a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "620f1829-4215-5cda-9dd0-aa2a1afa5537" :question/math-academy-id "q-124035" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ for $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(-1,\\pi )-f(1,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}-(-1))-({0}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)+1 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
